LVGL (RT1176 port) — attribution and third-party notices
========================================================

`LICENSE` is the MIT text that governs this repository. This file records who
holds copyright over what, and the licences of the third-party code bundled
inside the vendored tree. Both were previously spelled out inside `LICENSE`
itself; they moved here so that automated licence detection can identify the
repository (a file containing a preamble and two full licence texts matches no
known licence, and GitHub reported NOASSERTION — measured, not guessed).

Two bodies of work, both MIT
----------------------------

  * `lvgl/` — the vendored upstream LVGL tree.
    Copyright (c) 2025 LVGL Kft.

  * `port/`, and everything else at the top level of this repository — the
    i.MX RT1176 port.
    Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Newdigate.

The licence text is identical for both; only the attribution differs, which is
why `LICENSE` carries a single MIT body with both copyright lines. Upstream's
own licence file is also retained verbatim at `lvgl/LICENCE.txt` — that copy,
not this summary, is the authoritative reproduction of the upstream notice.

Which holder covers which path is recorded HERE and deliberately not in
`LICENSE`: automated detectors strip whole lines that match a copyright
pattern, so an annotation appended to a copyright line — or worse, wrapped onto
a continuation line — is read as licence body text and the match fails. That is
not hypothetical; it is why this repository still reported NOASSERTION after
the first attempt at this split.

`VENDORING.md` records the upstream provenance and the components pruned on
vendoring for licensing reasons.

