Apache-2.0 files (three), none of which contribute code here
------------------------------------------------------------

    lvgl/src/osal/lv_cmsis_rtos2.c
    lvgl/src/osal/lv_cmsis_rtos2.h
    lvgl/src/drivers/opengles/glad/include/EGL/eglplatform.h

The two headers are never included by this port. The `.c` IS compiled — the
consuming build globs `lvgl/src/**.c` — but its entire body sits behind
`#if LV_USE_OS == LV_OS_CMSIS_RTOS2`, and this port sets `LV_USE_OS 0`. Its
object therefore defines **zero symbols**, verified with `arm-none-eabi-nm
--defined-only`, so no Apache-2.0 code reaches any firmware image built from
this repository.

That verification is the point, not the conclusion. "It is behind an `#if`" is
an argument; an object file with no symbols in it is evidence. The consuming
tree's `tools/license-audit.sh` applies the same empty-object rule elsewhere
for the same reason. If a future `lv_conf.h` ever selects CMSIS-RTOS2, this
paragraph stops being true and Apache-2.0 code starts linking — re-run the
check rather than trusting this note.

Re-vendoring
------------

Re-run the surveys behind the two sections above and reconcile this file with
what they print:

    find lvgl -iname 'licen*' -o -iname 'copying*'
    grep -rhoE "SPDX-License-Identifier: *[A-Za-z0-9.+-]+" lvgl/src | sort | uniq -c

A copyleft grep alone is not sufficient. It answers "is there GPL/LGPL/MPL
here", which is a narrower question than "is this repository the licence it
claims" — a distinction that cost a day when a sibling repository advertised
"MIT throughout" while shipping two Apache-2.0 files.
